===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term/t|la|docēntem}}, present participle of {{term|docere|docēre|to teach}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈdəʊ.sənt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈdoʊ.sənt/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[instructive|Instructive]]; that teaches.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[teacher]] or [[lecturer]] at some American [[college]]s or [[universities]].
#* '''2006''', Thomas Pynchon, ''Against the Day'', Vintage 2007, p. 1212:
#*: Zermelo had been a '''docent''' at Göttingen when Kit was there and, like Russell, had been preoccupied with the set of all sets that are not members of themselves.
# {{context|chiefly|US|lang=en}} A [[tour]] [[guide]] at a [[museum]], [[art gallery]], historical site, etc.
#: ''The '''docent''' greeted the visitors and welcomed them to the Louvre.''

